 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 535063,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/fa40e613435e5ac1.jpg\" alt=\"An illustration without labels shows the kidney, shaped like a kidney bean standing on end. Two layers, the outer renal fascia and an inner capsule, cover the outside of the kidney. The inside of the kidney consists of three layers: the outer cortex, the middle medulla, and the inner renal pelvis. The renal pelvis is flush with the concave side of the kidney and empties into the ureter, a tube that runs down outside the concave side of the kidney. Nine renal pyramids are embedded in the medulla, which is the thickest kidney layer. Each renal pyramid is teardrop-shaped, with the narrow end facing the renal pelvis. The renal artery and renal vein enter the concave part of the kidney, just above the ureter. The renal artery and renal vein branch into arterioles and venules, respectively, which extend into the kidney and branch into capillaries in the cortex.\"></p>\n\n<p>The diagram shows the left kidney. Why do the capillaries carrying blood from the renal artery run over the top of the renal pyramids?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1046762,
     "content_html": "The capillaries deliver blood to the glomerulus and run parallel to the proximal convoluted tubule. Both are located in the medulla."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046763,
     "content_html": "The capillaries deliver blood to the glomerulus and run perpendicular to the proximal convoluted tubule. Both are located in the cortex."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046764,
     "content_html": "The capillaries deliver blood to the glomerulus and run perpendicular to the distal convoluted tubule. Both are located in the cortex."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046765,
     "content_html": "The capillaries deliver blood to the glomerulus and run parallel to the distal convoluted tubule. Both are located in the cortex."
    }
   ],
